{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current values $x$ and $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}+6\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}+3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+3\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}+3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-3\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-3\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62ea4f7cf4742f65ba91f3b52b78c97136b98d3e243bf520b22b8aa8c6cf95aa"} {"question" "q-334072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-3\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-3\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}^{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{3}-y-3\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-6\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-6\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-3\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62ea4f7cf4742f65ba91f3b52b78c97136b98d3e243bf520b22b8aa8c6cf95aa"} {"question" "q-334077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example  $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({x}^{2}-y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}-1+{2}^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}(0+4-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62ea4f7cf4742f65ba91f3b52b78c97136b98d3e243bf520b22b8aa8c6cf95aa"} {"question" "q-334080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-3"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-3"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-4"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+{y}^{3},\\,y(1)=-1.4\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})+({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.5{x}^{2}+0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}^{2}+0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+0.5{x}^{2}+0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+y-{y}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+0.5{x}^{2}+0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+y-{y}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+0.5(1)^{2}+0.5(-1.4)^{3}+0.5(2)^{2}-1.4-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+0.5-1.372+2-1.4-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.272.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-1.4$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.272}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1)=-1.4,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-1.4 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈-1.274\\,227 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-1.251\\,778 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-1.251\\,069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-1.25,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62ea4f7cf4742f65ba91f3b52b78c97136b98d3e243bf520b22b8aa8c6cf95aa"} {"question" "q-334449" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334449" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.25"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334472" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-{y}^{3},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334472" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\,Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.5{x}^{2}-0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+0.5{x}^{2}-0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-0.5{x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-0.5{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Then the function $g$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5(1)^{3}-1-0.5\\cdot {2}^{2}-0.5\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-3}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.600\\,00 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.466\\,116 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.456\\,216\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈1.46,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334472" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62ea4f7cf4742f65ba91f3b52b78c97136b98d3e243bf520b22b8aa8c6cf95aa"} {"question" "q-334472" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063570/state.json" "file_sha256" "db3d3216865e3c9055469b0ce53543394095f075e8b256bf2cc85a65b32afa2a"} {"question" "q-334472" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/